        "Four nerves participate in providing cutaneous supply to the neck: the supraclavicular, great auricular, transverse cervical, and lesser occipital nerves.\n\n<b>Great auricular nerve (C2–C3)</b> — supplies the skin over the angle of the jaw and the area posteroinferior to the auricle of the ear.\n\n<b>Other cutaneous nerves of the neck:</b>\n- Transverse cervical nerve (C2–C3) — passes anteriorly and supplies the anterior and lateral aspects of the neck.\n- Lesser occipital nerve — supplies the skin of the back of the neck and posterior occiput.\n- Supraclavicular nerves (C3–C4) — supply the inferior aspects of the neck, upper deltoid region, and skin inferior to the clavicles.",
