      correct: 2,
      explanation:
        "The most likely structures encountered during a midline incision below the thyroid isthmus are the <b>inferior thyroid vein and thyroidea ima artery</b>.\n\n- The inferior thyroid veins typically drain the thyroid gland into the left brachiocephalic vein, which crosses superficially just inferior to the thyroid isthmus.\n- The thyroidea ima artery is an inconsistent vessel that may arise from the aortic arch, vertebral artery, or another source.\n- The middle thyroid veins drain the thyroid gland into the internal jugular vein and are located superior to the incision site.\n- The inferior thyroid arteries arise from the subclavian arteries and approach the thyroid gland obliquely; they are not typically encountered in a midline incision.\n- The brachiocephalic veins lie inferior to the incision site.",
