      explanation:
        "The <b>vagus nerve (CN X)</b> exits the skull through the jugular foramen. It provides motor innervation to the muscles of the palate, pharynx, larynx, and superior two-thirds of the esophagus, as well as smooth muscle in the trachea, bronchi, and digestive tract. Injury can therefore cause dysphagia and aspiration.\n\n- The ansa cervicalis innervates the infrahyoid strap muscles, except the thyrohyoid muscle.\n- The cervical sympathetic trunk does not pass through the jugular foramen. It runs posterior to the carotid sheath, parallel to the internal carotid artery. Its carotid branches carry sympathetic fibers into the head.\n- Injury to the external laryngeal nerve paralyzes the cricothyroid muscle and may cause vocal fatigue and hoarseness.\n- Injury to the hypoglossal nerve causes the tongue to deviate toward the affected side on protrusion and may produce dysarthria.",
