      explanation:
        "The retropharyngeal space extends from the inferior aspect of the skull to the posterior mediastinum behind the esophagus. An infection or abscess in this space can therefore spread toward the posterior mediastinum.\n\nThe retropharyngeal space is enclosed between the visceral fascia covering the posterior wall of the pharynx (buccopharyngeal fascia) and the alar layer of the prevertebral fascia. The alar fascia is formed from bilateral anterior extensions of the prevertebral fascia.\n\nBetween the alar fascia and the more posterior prevertebral fascia is the danger space of the neck. This space extends from the base of the skull through the posterior mediastinum to the respiratory diaphragm.\n\nThe alar fascia is continuous with the carotid sheath and forms the posterior boundary of the retropharyngeal space. The prevertebral fascia covers the vertebral column and intrinsic back muscles. The pretracheal fascia encloses the trachea and larynx, whereas the buccopharyngeal fascia covers the superior pharyngeal constrictor and buccinator muscles.",
