        "The recurrent laryngeal nerve, a branch of the vagus nerve (CN X), supplies motor innervation to all intrinsic muscles of the larynx except the cricothyroid muscle. It also supplies sensory fibers to the larynx below the vocal cords.\n\nThe left recurrent laryngeal nerve hooks under the ligamentum arteriosum at the aortic arch, whereas the right nerve hooks under the right subclavian artery. Both nerves ascend toward the larynx through the tracheoesophageal groove and pass posterior to the middle part of the thyroid gland. Therefore, they may be inadvertently damaged during thyroidectomy, causing hoarseness.\n\nThe internal laryngeal nerve supplies sensory fibers to the laryngeal mucosa above the vocal cords; injury causes anesthesia in this region. The external laryngeal nerve supplies the cricothyroid muscle, which tenses the vocal cords. Its paralysis causes a monotonous voice due to inability to tense the vocal cords. Injury to the superior laryngeal nerve can also cause sensory loss above the vocal cords and loss of the protective laryngeal reflex.",
